1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,613
Positive Behaviour Support Final Assignment.

2
00:00:03,563 --> 00:00:07,302
S D 41 Education Assistant Program.

3
00:00:07,302 --> 00:00:09,118
Brooklyn Case Study.

4
00:00:09,118 --> 00:00:12,221
Prepared by Peter, Sue and Sarah.

5
00:00:12,221 --> 00:00:15,460
September 6th, 2026.

6
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:16,959
Section one.

7
00:00:16,959 --> 00:00:18,921
Background Information.

8
00:00:18,921 --> 00:00:26,342
Brooklyn is 16 and lives with foster parents and their children, who include him in family and community activities.

9
00:00:26,342 --> 00:00:35,355
Monthly visits with his biological mother are difficult for him, and may be followed by more intense behaviour at home and a more difficult week at school.

10
00:00:35,355 --> 00:00:40,842
Both his foster family and school team are supportive and want what is best for Brooklyn.

11
00:00:40,842 --> 00:00:42,392
Section two.

12
00:00:42,392 --> 00:00:44,184
School Information.

13
00:00:44,184 --> 00:00:46,169
Preferred activities.

14
00:00:46,169 --> 00:00:58,605
Brooklyn enjoys community outings, his weekly recycling job and the Slurpee that follows, cooking and laundry activities, business cards and brochures, and carrying his daily planner to classes.

15
00:00:58,605 --> 00:00:59,855
Strengths.

16
00:00:59,855 --> 00:01:08,434
Brooklyn is successful with repetitive tasks such as folding laundry, stocking shelves, wiping tables, and sorting recycling.

17
00:01:08,434 --> 00:01:18,421
He takes responsibility for the weekly recycling job, participates in community activities, and is learning to use a daily planner to organize his school day.

18
00:01:18,421 --> 00:01:20,328
Areas of difficulty.

19
00:01:20,328 --> 00:01:26,381
Brooklyn has difficulty regulating strong emotions following stressful family contact.

20
00:01:26,381 --> 00:01:37,197
At school, he has difficulty accepting limits around business cards, interacting with Teddy during reading, and completing handwriting tasks because of poor fine motor skills.

21
00:01:37,197 --> 00:01:42,526
He may avoid journal writing by staying in the washroom for up to 25 minutes.

22
00:01:42,526 --> 00:01:45,447
The specific concerns are as follows.

23
00:01:45,447 --> 00:01:46,342
One.

24
00:01:46,342 --> 00:01:50,460
Strong emotions after visits with his biological mother.

25
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:52,223
Self-regulation.

26
00:01:52,223 --> 00:01:53,092
Two.

27
00:01:53,092 --> 00:01:59,511
Taking all the business cards when the display is full, and sometimes becoming angry when escorted out.

28
00:01:59,511 --> 00:02:02,552
Self-regulation, and communication.

29
00:02:02,552 --> 00:02:03,526
Three.

30
00:02:03,526 --> 00:02:08,368
Appearing anxious around Teddy, and yelling when Teddy asks what is wrong.

31
00:02:08,368 --> 00:02:12,539
Self-regulation, social skills and communication.

32
00:02:12,539 --> 00:02:13,537
Four.

33
00:02:13,537 --> 00:02:17,802
Holding a pen or pencil, because of poor fine motor skills.

34
00:02:17,802 --> 00:02:24,131
When asked to write, he may spend 25 minutes or more in the washroom and miss much of journal time.

35
00:02:24,131 --> 00:02:27,381
Fine motor skills, and task management.

36
00:02:27,381 --> 00:02:28,934
Section three.

37
00:02:28,934 --> 00:02:30,919
Behaviour Pathways.

38
00:02:30,919 --> 00:02:32,353
Pathway one.

39
00:02:32,353 --> 00:02:33,828
Business cards.

40
00:02:33,828 --> 00:02:35,368
Setting event.

41
00:02:35,368 --> 00:02:40,064
Brooklyn is on a community walk and visits the town's visitor centre.

42
00:02:40,064 --> 00:02:43,763
He has a strong interest in business cards and brochures.

43
00:02:43,763 --> 00:02:50,131
He spends a long time looking at and reading them, and likes the idea of a job where he can hand out a card.

44
00:02:50,131 --> 00:02:55,315
There may also have been a difficult week after a visit with his biological mother.

45
00:02:55,315 --> 00:02:56,815
Antecedent.

46
00:02:56,815 --> 00:03:01,736
Brooklyn enters the visitor centre and sees a full display of business cards.

47
00:03:01,736 --> 00:03:03,037
Behaviour.

48
00:03:03,037 --> 00:03:07,958
Brooklyn takes many or all of the business cards and puts them in his pockets.

49
00:03:07,958 --> 00:03:09,342
Consequence.

50
00:03:09,342 --> 00:03:13,815
The Education Assistant escorts Brooklyn out of the visitor centre.

51
00:03:13,815 --> 00:03:16,684
This can sometimes make him very angry.

52
00:03:16,684 --> 00:03:18,342
Possible function.

53
00:03:18,342 --> 00:03:20,315
Seeking business cards.

54
00:03:20,315 --> 00:03:21,907
Lagging skills.

55
00:03:21,907 --> 00:03:27,355
Social skills, communication, self-regulation, and self-control.

56
00:03:27,355 --> 00:03:29,394
Alternative behaviour.

57
00:03:29,394 --> 00:03:44,750
Brooklyn will ask, may I choose three cards, or show a request card to get three business cards, with safe hands, calmly, and leave the booth without being escorted outside of the visitor centre, one hundred percent of the time, by February 2027.

58
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:46,616
Desired behaviour.

59
00:03:46,616 --> 00:03:57,723
Brooklyn will independently participate in the visitor centre visit by browsing the displays, following the centre's rules about taking materials, and leaving with the group at the scheduled time.

60
00:03:57,723 --> 00:04:03,736
This includes times when business cards are unavailable, or permission to take them is not given.

61
00:04:03,736 --> 00:04:14,842
He will do this without grabbing cards, and without requiring an individual Education Assistant escort, in at least four out of five consecutive visits, by June 2027.

62
00:04:14,842 --> 00:04:16,353
Pathway two.

63
00:04:16,353 --> 00:04:18,289
Reading group with Teddy.

64
00:04:18,289 --> 00:04:19,828
Setting event.

65
00:04:19,828 --> 00:04:23,355
Brooklyn and Teddy are placed in the same reading group.

66
00:04:23,355 --> 00:04:26,392
Brooklyn appears anxious when they are together.

67
00:04:26,392 --> 00:04:30,723
There may also have been a monthly visit with his biological mother.

68
00:04:30,723 --> 00:04:32,223
Antecedent.

69
00:04:32,223 --> 00:04:35,947
Teddy asks Brooklyn what is wrong during reading time.

70
00:04:35,947 --> 00:04:37,234
Behaviour.

71
00:04:37,234 --> 00:04:40,671
Brooklyn yells at Teddy at a very high volume.

72
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:42,323
Consequence.

73
00:04:42,273 --> 00:04:49,378
The Education Assistant or teacher separates Brooklyn and Teddy, breaks up the group, and reads to them separately.

74
00:04:49,378 --> 00:04:51,036
Possible function.

75
00:04:51,036 --> 00:04:55,326
Avoiding Teddy, and seeking Education Assistant attention.

76
00:04:55,326 --> 00:04:56,918
Lagging skills.

77
00:04:56,918 --> 00:05:02,668
Social skills, self-regulation, communication, and executive function.

78
00:05:02,668 --> 00:05:04,707
Alternative behaviour.

79
00:05:04,707 --> 00:05:21,273
When Teddy asks Brooklyn what is wrong during reading time, Brooklyn will calmly say, I need space, show a space or break card, or ask an adult for help, to obtain space or a brief break from the interaction, one hundred percent of the time, by February 2027.

80
00:05:21,273 --> 00:05:23,140
Desired behaviour.

81
00:05:23,140 --> 00:05:41,181
During reading group with Teddy, Brooklyn will independently respond to Teddy's question by answering, or by saying, I don't want to answer, using a conversational voice, and complete his assigned portion of the reading task while remaining in the group, eighty-five percent of the time, by June 2027.

82
00:05:41,181 --> 00:05:42,707
Pathway three.

83
00:05:42,707 --> 00:05:44,221
Journal writing.

84
00:05:44,221 --> 00:05:45,760
Setting event.

85
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:50,786
It is hard for Brooklyn to hold the pencil, because of poor fine motor skills.

86
00:05:50,786 --> 00:05:55,181
There may also have been a monthly visit with his biological mother.

87
00:05:55,181 --> 00:05:56,681
Antecedent.

88
00:05:56,681 --> 00:05:59,786
Brooklyn is asked to write his journal daily.

89
00:05:59,786 --> 00:06:01,087
Behaviour.

90
00:06:01,087 --> 00:06:06,418
Brooklyn asks to use the washroom, and may stay there for up to 25 minutes.

91
00:06:06,418 --> 00:06:07,839
Consequence.

92
00:06:07,839 --> 00:06:14,655
The Education Assistant knocks and tells him to hurry, and Brooklyn misses a large portion of the journal block.

93
00:06:14,655 --> 00:06:16,326
Possible function.

94
00:06:16,326 --> 00:06:18,484
Avoiding journal writing.

95
00:06:18,484 --> 00:06:20,076
Lagging skills.

96
00:06:20,076 --> 00:06:26,023
Fine motor skills, self-regulation, communication, and executive function.

97
00:06:26,023 --> 00:06:28,063
Alternative behaviour.

98
00:06:28,063 --> 00:06:44,563
When asked to write in his journal, Brooklyn will say, I need help, or, I need a break, or show a help or break card, to request assistance, an adapted writing option such as typing, or a brief break, one hundred percent of the time, by February 2027.

99
00:06:44,563 --> 00:06:46,429
Desired behaviour.

100
00:06:46,429 --> 00:07:03,179
During journal writing, Brooklyn will independently begin and complete his agreed upon portions of the task, using an accessible writing method such as typing or an adapted pencil, and return to the task after planned breaks, eighty-five percent of the time, by June 2027.

101
00:07:03,179 --> 00:07:04,628
Section four.

102
00:07:04,628 --> 00:07:07,247
Strategies and Interventions.

103
00:07:07,247 --> 00:07:08,719
Pathway one.

104
00:07:08,719 --> 00:07:10,194
Business cards.

105
00:07:10,194 --> 00:07:14,903
Preventative and antecedent strategies, for the setting event.

106
00:07:14,903 --> 00:07:19,087
Consider not visiting the visitor centre on difficult weeks.

107
00:07:19,087 --> 00:07:23,418
Frontload with a social story about not grabbing all the cards.

108
00:07:23,418 --> 00:07:26,221
Frontload with a contingency map.

109
00:07:26,221 --> 00:07:31,510
Frontload about the location being visited, with pictures of the visitor centre.

110
00:07:31,510 --> 00:07:37,681
Use first and then, for calming down and asking for more, with an agreed upon number of cards.

111
00:07:37,681 --> 00:07:41,931
First take the agreed upon number of cards, then leave the booth.

112
00:07:41,931 --> 00:07:46,063
He can take three cards at the centre, and print more at school.

113
00:07:46,063 --> 00:07:50,811
Preventative and antecedent strategies, for the antecedent.

114
00:07:50,811 --> 00:07:52,811
Token board rewarding.

115
00:07:52,811 --> 00:07:54,997
A calming down strategy.

116
00:07:54,997 --> 00:07:56,878
A choice of location.

117
00:07:56,878 --> 00:08:00,497
A choice of the number of cards he is allowed to collect.

118
00:08:00,497 --> 00:08:03,813
Frontload about how others feel and behave.

119
00:08:03,813 --> 00:08:05,798
Teaching strategies.

120
00:08:05,798 --> 00:08:08,153
Teach requesting and waiting.

121
00:08:08,153 --> 00:08:12,865
Model saying, may I choose three cards, or showing a request card.

122
00:08:12,865 --> 00:08:18,642
Use role play to practise asking before touching the display, and waiting for permission.

123
00:08:18,642 --> 00:08:20,772
Teach the collection limit.

124
00:08:20,772 --> 00:08:28,457
Use a three space counting mat to practise selecting up to three permitted cards, and leaving the remaining cards in the display.

125
00:08:28,457 --> 00:08:30,298
Teach flexibility.

126
00:08:30,298 --> 00:08:37,127
Practise situations where cards are available, where fewer cards are permitted, or where no cards are available.

127
00:08:37,127 --> 00:08:42,286
Model requesting a brief break, or choosing to make personal business cards later.

128
00:08:42,286 --> 00:08:44,089
Teach the transition.

129
00:08:44,089 --> 00:08:47,048
Practise following the visual sequence.

130
00:08:47,048 --> 00:08:48,036
Ask.

131
00:08:48,036 --> 00:08:49,010
Wait.

132
00:08:49,010 --> 00:08:50,061
Choose.

133
00:08:50,061 --> 00:08:51,681
Rejoin the group.

134
00:08:51,681 --> 00:08:56,681
Begin with adult modelling and prompts, then gradually reduce assistance.

135
00:08:56,681 --> 00:08:58,971
Materials and example.

136
00:08:58,971 --> 00:09:04,984
Use practice business cards, a request card, a counting mat, and a visual sequence.

137
00:09:04,984 --> 00:09:09,061
During practice, the adult says, you may take two.

138
00:09:09,061 --> 00:09:12,944
Brooklyn selects two cards and rejoins the activity.

139
00:09:12,944 --> 00:09:15,126
Consequence strategies.

140
00:09:15,126 --> 00:09:17,918
Reinforce appropriate requests.

141
00:09:17,918 --> 00:09:22,615
Respond promptly when Brooklyn asks, or uses his request card.

142
00:09:22,615 --> 00:09:27,036
If staff give permission, allow him to collect the permitted cards.

143
00:09:27,268 --> 00:09:29,308
Give specific praise.

144
00:09:29,258 --> 00:09:33,378
For example, you waited for permission and took two cards.

145
00:09:33,378 --> 00:09:36,206
Reinforce broader participation.

146
00:09:36,206 --> 00:09:43,089
If using a token board, award tokens for agreed skills, such as following the limit and leaving with the group.

147
00:09:43,089 --> 00:09:45,878
Provide the agreed reward when earned.

148
00:09:45,878 --> 00:09:48,181
Respond calmly to grabbing.

149
00:09:48,181 --> 00:09:53,181
Support Brooklyn to return extra cards, and prompt the request routine.

150
00:09:53,181 --> 00:09:55,060
Keep language brief.

151
00:09:55,060 --> 00:09:56,812
Support recovery.

152
00:09:56,812 --> 00:10:01,575
If Brooklyn becomes upset, offer space and regulation support.

153
00:10:01,575 --> 00:10:05,008
Practise the skill again when he is calm and ready.

154
00:10:05,008 --> 00:10:06,481
Pathway two.

155
00:10:06,481 --> 00:10:08,404
Reading group with Teddy.

156
00:10:08,404 --> 00:10:13,126
Preventative and antecedent strategies, for the setting event.

157
00:10:13,126 --> 00:10:13,983
One.

158
00:10:13,983 --> 00:10:15,982
Choice of group members.

159
00:10:15,982 --> 00:10:19,904
Where possible, allow Brooklyn to choose a reading partner.

160
00:10:19,904 --> 00:10:24,602
Gradually introduce short, supported reading activities with Teddy.

161
00:10:24,602 --> 00:10:25,470
Two.

162
00:10:25,470 --> 00:10:26,904
Plan breaks.

163
00:10:26,904 --> 00:10:32,312
Arrange a brief break option, and practise requesting space before reading time.

164
00:10:32,312 --> 00:10:33,312
Three.

165
00:10:33,312 --> 00:10:35,536
Reduce the initial demand.

166
00:10:35,536 --> 00:10:40,760
Start with a smaller reading portion, or a shorter period of group work with Teddy.

167
00:10:40,760 --> 00:10:41,758
Four.

168
00:10:41,758 --> 00:10:44,286
Frontload with a social story.

169
00:10:44,286 --> 00:10:48,773
Review how to respond when someone asks a question or disagrees.

170
00:10:48,773 --> 00:10:54,760
Explain that Brooklyn can answer, respectfully decline to answer, or request space.

171
00:10:54,760 --> 00:10:59,521
Preventative and antecedent strategies, for the antecedent.

172
00:10:59,521 --> 00:11:00,391
One.

173
00:11:00,391 --> 00:11:02,733
Review a contingency map.

174
00:11:02,733 --> 00:11:05,337
Before the interaction, show.

175
00:11:05,337 --> 00:11:08,483
Use words or a card to ask for space.

176
00:11:08,483 --> 00:11:10,115
Receive space.

177
00:11:10,115 --> 00:11:12,970
Return to the reading task when ready.

178
00:11:12,970 --> 00:11:13,839
Two.

179
00:11:13,839 --> 00:11:16,154
Offer a choice of location.

180
00:11:16,154 --> 00:11:20,931
Let Brooklyn choose between suitable seats with enough personal space.

181
00:11:20,931 --> 00:11:21,931
Three.

182
00:11:21,931 --> 00:11:23,707
Use a soft start.

183
00:11:23,707 --> 00:11:28,692
Begin group work with a short, familiar or preferred reading activity.

184
00:11:28,692 --> 00:11:29,652
Four.

185
00:11:29,652 --> 00:11:31,931
Provide a familiar support.

186
00:11:31,931 --> 00:11:36,931
Make a favourite calming object available if it helps Brooklyn participate.

187
00:11:36,931 --> 00:11:38,008
Five.

188
00:11:38,008 --> 00:11:40,363
Prompt communication early.

189
00:11:40,363 --> 00:11:50,562
If Teddy asks a question and Brooklyn appears uncomfortable, point to his space card, or remind him that he can say, I don't want to answer, or, I need space.

190
00:11:50,562 --> 00:11:52,534
Teaching strategies.

191
00:11:52,534 --> 00:11:55,152
Teach requesting space or help.

192
00:11:55,152 --> 00:11:57,233
Model, I need space.

193
00:11:57,233 --> 00:11:58,694
I need a break.

194
00:11:58,694 --> 00:12:00,694
And, can you help me.

195
00:12:00,694 --> 00:12:04,850
Practise using the matching cards as communication options.

196
00:12:04,850 --> 00:12:07,231
Teach responding to questions.

197
00:12:07,231 --> 00:12:13,733
Role play answering a question, or saying, I don't want to answer, using a conversational voice.

198
00:12:13,733 --> 00:12:18,233
Make clear that Brooklyn does not have to disclose personal feelings.

199
00:12:18,233 --> 00:12:20,797
Teach recognizing discomfort.

200
00:12:20,797 --> 00:12:29,733
Help Brooklyn identify his own early signs of discomfort, and choose a familiar strategy, such as breathing slowly or requesting space.

201
00:12:29,733 --> 00:12:31,733
Teach returning to reading.

202
00:12:31,733 --> 00:12:34,034
Practise a short sequence.

203
00:12:34,034 --> 00:12:35,589
Request a break.

204
00:12:35,589 --> 00:12:37,048
Take the break.

205
00:12:37,048 --> 00:12:40,431
Return to the next manageable reading portion.

206
00:12:40,431 --> 00:12:42,641
Gradually reduce prompts.

207
00:12:42,641 --> 00:12:44,931
Materials and example.

208
00:12:44,931 --> 00:12:50,957
Use communication cards, a voice volume visual, a timer, and a reading checklist.

209
00:12:50,957 --> 00:12:54,023
A trusted adult asks, what's wrong.

210
00:12:54,023 --> 00:12:59,970
Brooklyn practises declining to answer and continuing to read, or requesting space.

211
00:12:59,970 --> 00:13:02,166
Consequence strategies.

212
00:13:02,166 --> 00:13:04,786
Honour appropriate communication.

213
00:13:04,786 --> 00:13:10,323
Provide space, a brief break, or adult assistance when Brooklyn requests it.

214
00:13:10,323 --> 00:13:13,075
Respect his decision not to answer.

215
00:13:13,075 --> 00:13:15,113
Give specific praise.

216
00:13:15,113 --> 00:13:18,839
For example, you used your words to ask for space.

217
00:13:18,839 --> 00:13:21,244
Reinforce participation.

218
00:13:21,244 --> 00:13:25,957
Acknowledge returning after a break, and completing his reading portion.

219
00:13:25,957 --> 00:13:30,720
If a token system is used, award tokens for these agreed actions.

220
00:13:30,720 --> 00:13:32,996
Respond calmly to yelling.

221
00:13:32,996 --> 00:13:37,602
Reduce verbal demands, and help both students obtain enough space.

222
00:13:37,602 --> 00:13:42,115
Offer a familiar communication option when Brooklyn can use it.

223
00:13:42,115 --> 00:13:43,812
Support re-entry.

224
00:13:43,812 --> 00:13:50,023
Once Brooklyn is ready, offer an achievable reading step, and support his return to the group.

225
00:13:50,023 --> 00:13:53,918
Review the interaction privately after he has settled.

226
00:13:53,918 --> 00:13:55,457
Pathway three.

227
00:13:55,457 --> 00:13:56,970
Journal writing.

228
00:13:56,970 --> 00:14:01,692
Preventative and antecedent strategies, for the setting event.

229
00:14:01,692 --> 00:14:02,549
One.

230
00:14:02,549 --> 00:14:05,166
Prepare a choice of materials.

231
00:14:05,166 --> 00:14:10,508
Provide accessible options, such as typing, or using an adapted pencil.

232
00:14:10,508 --> 00:14:11,352
Two.

233
00:14:11,352 --> 00:14:13,325
Allow additional time.

234
00:14:13,325 --> 00:14:18,891
Plan enough time for Brooklyn to complete his agreed upon writing portions without rushing.

235
00:14:18,891 --> 00:14:19,891
Three.

236
00:14:19,891 --> 00:14:22,705
Plan seating and support choices.

237
00:14:22,705 --> 00:14:27,628
Offer suitable options for where Brooklyn works, and whom he sits beside.

238
00:14:27,916 --> 00:14:28,916
Four.

239
00:14:28,866 --> 00:14:31,342
Include preferred materials.

240
00:14:31,342 --> 00:14:36,487
Make preferred pencil or text colours available, to encourage engagement.

241
00:14:36,487 --> 00:14:37,526
Five.

242
00:14:37,526 --> 00:14:39,342
Adjust the workload.

243
00:14:39,342 --> 00:14:44,763
Plan a small, achievable starting portion, and brief breaks between portions.

244
00:14:44,763 --> 00:14:45,842
Six.

245
00:14:45,842 --> 00:14:48,408
Frontload with a social story.

246
00:14:48,408 --> 00:14:55,710
Before journal time, review starting work, asking for help, taking a break, and returning to the task.

247
00:14:55,710 --> 00:15:00,459
Preventative and antecedent strategies, for the antecedent.

248
00:15:00,459 --> 00:15:01,329
One.

249
00:15:01,329 --> 00:15:03,408
Show a visual schedule.

250
00:15:03,408 --> 00:15:06,329
At the start of journal time, review.

251
00:15:06,329 --> 00:15:07,947
Write one part.

252
00:15:07,947 --> 00:15:09,276
Brief break.

253
00:15:09,276 --> 00:15:11,092
Write the next part.

254
00:15:11,092 --> 00:15:12,250
Finished.

255
00:15:12,250 --> 00:15:13,118
Two.

256
00:15:13,118 --> 00:15:15,092
Confirm his choices.

257
00:15:15,092 --> 00:15:21,658
Ask Brooklyn to select his writing method, colour, and work location from the available options.

258
00:15:21,658 --> 00:15:22,658
Three.

259
00:15:22,658 --> 00:15:25,248
Present one portion at a time.

260
00:15:25,248 --> 00:15:31,750
Give a manageable instruction, such as, write one sentence, with a sentence starter if needed.

261
00:15:31,750 --> 00:15:32,748
Four.

262
00:15:32,748 --> 00:15:34,723
Preview the token board.

263
00:15:34,723 --> 00:15:41,447
Explain the specific action that earns a token, such as beginning or completing an agreed upon portion.

264
00:15:41,447 --> 00:15:44,539
Award the token after the action occurs.

265
00:15:44,539 --> 00:15:45,579
Five.

266
00:15:45,579 --> 00:15:48,276
Remind him how to request support.

267
00:15:48,276 --> 00:15:54,300
Keep help and break cards within reach, and say, you can ask for help, or a brief break.

268
00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:56,879
Keep washroom access available.

269
00:15:56,879 --> 00:15:58,827
Teaching strategies.

270
00:15:58,827 --> 00:16:00,945
Teach requesting support.

271
00:16:00,945 --> 00:16:06,500
Model saying, I need help, or, I need a break, or showing the matching card.

272
00:16:06,500 --> 00:16:11,564
Practise during easy tasks before using the skill during journal writing.

273
00:16:11,564 --> 00:16:14,261
Teach an accessible writing method.

274
00:16:14,261 --> 00:16:19,816
Explicitly teach the steps Brooklyn needs to type, or to use an adapted pencil.

275
00:16:19,816 --> 00:16:24,934
Model one step, practise together, and gradually reduce assistance.

276
00:16:24,934 --> 00:16:28,103
Teach task initiation and completion.

277
00:16:28,103 --> 00:16:29,723
Use a checklist.

278
00:16:29,723 --> 00:16:31,301
Choose a topic.

279
00:16:31,301 --> 00:16:32,880
Say my idea.

280
00:16:32,880 --> 00:16:35,039
Write or type one part.

281
00:16:35,039 --> 00:16:36,392
Check it off.

282
00:16:36,392 --> 00:16:38,855
Begin with a manageable portion.

283
00:16:38,855 --> 00:16:41,353
Teach returning after breaks.

284
00:16:41,353 --> 00:16:47,129
Practise checking the visual schedule, and restarting at the next step after a planned break.

285
00:16:47,129 --> 00:16:49,394
Materials and example.

286
00:16:49,394 --> 00:16:57,237
Use a keyboard or adapted pencil, communication cards, sentence starters, a visual schedule, and a timer.

287
00:16:57,237 --> 00:17:03,526
Brooklyn types one sentence, requests a brief break, and returns to the next agreed portion.

288
00:17:03,526 --> 00:17:05,735
Consequence strategies.

289
00:17:05,735 --> 00:17:07,816
Respond to requests.

290
00:17:07,816 --> 00:17:14,919
Provide help, an adapted writing option, or a brief break, when Brooklyn appropriately requests support.

291
00:17:14,919 --> 00:17:16,932
Give specific praise.

292
00:17:16,932 --> 00:17:21,171
For example, you asked for help and completed your sentence.

293
00:17:21,171 --> 00:17:23,908
Reinforce achievable progress.

294
00:17:23,908 --> 00:17:32,500
If using a token board, award tokens for agreed actions, such as starting, completing a portion, or returning after a break.

295
00:17:32,500 --> 00:17:35,289
Provide the agreed reward when earned.

296
00:17:35,289 --> 00:17:38,210
Respond to difficulty supportively.

297
00:17:38,210 --> 00:17:46,355
If Brooklyn avoids or stops writing, check whether the task or writing tool needs adjustment, and prompt an appropriate request.

298
00:17:46,355 --> 00:17:50,171
Respond discreetly to extended washroom visits.

299
00:17:50,171 --> 00:17:52,840
Keep washroom access available.

300
00:17:52,840 --> 00:17:58,485
Check on his wellbeing according to school procedures, without repeated pressure to hurry.

301
00:17:58,485 --> 00:18:04,552
After he returns, provide a manageable next step, and the support needed to complete it.

302
00:18:04,552 --> 00:18:06,221
Section five.

303
00:18:06,221 --> 00:18:09,221
Progress Monitoring and Data Collection.

